title: "Can Small Language Models Review Research Papers? Leniency, Prompt Sensitivity, and Agreement with Human Reviewers on ICLR 2024 and 2025"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Chaudhary
    given-names: Pawan
    affiliation: Dakota State University
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-10-11
repository-code: "https://github.com/absurdblady/small-llm-reviewers"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23299279
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.23299280
    description: "The archived v1.0 release on Zenodo."
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  Four small open language models (Qwen2.5 1.5B, 3B, 7B and Phi-3.5-mini) rate 400 ICLR 2024 and 2025
  submissions from their title and abstract, using four prompts that ask the same question in different words.
  The study measures how lenient the models are, how much the wording changes their verdict, how close they
  come to a single human reviewer, and what they reward. Includes code, prompts, model outputs with per-score
  probabilities, analysis, and the paper.
keywords:
  - large language models
  - LLM-as-a-judge
  - peer review
  - prompt sensitivity
  - evaluation
